check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5742649Z Note: a multi-user installation is possible. See https://nix.dev/manual/nix/stable/installation/installing-binary.html#multi-user-installation
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5749370Z ^[[1;31mSwitching to the Multi-user Installer^[[0m
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5830768Z ^[[32mWelcome to the Multi-User Nix Installation
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5838751Z ^[[0m
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5839093Z This installation tool will set up your computer with the Nix package
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5839688Z manager. This will happen in a few stages:
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5839912Z 
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5840124Z 1. Make sure your computer doesn't already have Nix. If it does, I
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5840713Z    will show you instructions on how to clean up your old install.
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5840983Z 
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5841183Z 2. Show you what I am going to install and where. Then I will ask
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5841591Z    if you are ready to continue.
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5841817Z 
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5842511Z 3. Create the system users ^[[34m(uids [30001..30008])^[[0m and groups ^[[34m(gid 30000)^[[0m
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5843065Z    that the Nix daemon uses to run builds. To create system users
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5843482Z    in a different range, exit and run this tool again with
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5843773Z    NIX_FIRST_BUILD_UID set.
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5843990Z 
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5844119Z 4. Perform the basic installation of the Nix files daemon.
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5844268Z 
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5844404Z 5. Configure your shell to import special Nix Profile files, so you
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5844632Z    can use Nix.
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5844721Z 
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5844794Z 6. Start the Nix daemon.
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5844895Z 
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5845079Z ^[[32m^[[4;32mWould you like to see a more detailed list of what I will do?
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5845357Z ^[[0mNo TTY, assuming you would say yes :)
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5849618Z 
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5850096Z I will:
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5850279Z 
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5850569Z  - make sure your computer doesn't already have Nix files
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5850977Z    (if it does, I will tell you how to clean them up.)
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5851409Z  - create local users (see the list above for the users I'll make)
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5851774Z  - create a local group (nixbld)
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5852080Z  - install Nix in /nix
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5852388Z  - create a configuration file in /etc/nix
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5852795Z  - set up the "default profile" by creating some Nix-related files in
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5853179Z    /root
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5863266Z  - back up /etc/bash.bashrc to /etc/bash.bashrc.backup-before-nix
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5863739Z  - update /etc/bash.bashrc to include some Nix configuration
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5873307Z  - load and start a service (at /etc/systemd/system/nix-daemon.service
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5873785Z    and /etc/systemd/system/nix-daemon.socket) for nix-daemon
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5874042Z 
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5875297Z ^[[32m^[[4;32mReady to continue?
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5875793Z ^[[0mNo TTY, assuming you would say yes :)
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5891110Z 
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5891628Z ^[[34m---- let's talk about sudo -----------------------------------------------------
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5899671Z ^[[0mThis script is going to call sudo a lot. Normally, it would show you
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5900095Z exactly what commands it is running and why. However, the script is
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5900349Z run in a headless fashion, like this:
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5900757Z 
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5900886Z   $ curl -L https://nixos.org/nix/install | sh
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5901020Z 
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5901151Z or maybe in a CI pipeline. Because of that, I'm going to skip the
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5901402Z verbose output in the interest of brevity.
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5901614Z 
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5901725Z If you would like to
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5901908Z see the output, try like this:
check (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T02:53:25.5902020Z 
